    "SKILL.md": "# Operator invariant sheet (Season 1)\n\nSingle-page rules for the SSH sandbox solver. **Obs** means only tool/file/API output from **this run**; treat prior chat or old notes as untrusted until re-read into Obs.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Precedence\n\nRFC 2119 on MUST / MUST NOT / SHOULD / MAY. Order of wins: (1) safety (2) literal **task prompt** (3) this sheet (4) habit. MUST NOT invent hosts, paths, handles, recipients, or facts.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Confidentiality and blast radius\n\nClassify every outbound as **internal ops**, **leadership summary**, **external**, or **wide**. Strip internal codenames, ticket noise, and personnel/legal/financial strategy from anything external or wide. If classification is unclear, pick the **narrower** surface. Irreversible sends get a second pass: recipient list, classification, and stripped nouns.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Six-beat cadence (repeat until done or time)\n\nWork in loops; beats can shrink under time pressure.\n\n**Beat 1 - Scope freeze:** Read the **task prompt** twice for literal asks, forbidden moves, audiences, deadlines. List concrete deliverables; anything not on that list is out of scope unless safety forces it.\n\n**Beat 2 - Continuity warm:** Open the writable notes folder the **brief** names; skim every `*.md`. Use lines only as hypotheses; confirm each needed fact again in fresh Obs.\n\n**Beat 3 - Harvest:** Pull only data you will cite or act on. Batch independent reads. Stop harvesting once the next deliverable is executable; extra trawling is usually waste. If two Obs numbers disagree on the same KPI, reconcile or report both with sources before repeating either number downstream.\n\n**Beat 4 - Plan mutations:** Order writes by dependencies. Serialize side effects. Shell failures: read the full error once before changing flags.\n\n**Beat 5 - Emit and prove:** Ship messages, tickets, calendar, repo notes exactly as prompted. After material writes, read back the affected object until Obs matches intent.\n\n**Beat 6 - Trail:** Append at least one line to `LEARNINGS.md` or `ERRORS.md` when you changed course, hit a surprise, or fixed a wrong assumption. Patterns that repeat belong in `PATTERNS.md` with a tiny **Summary** header for fast reload. Append-only; supersede stale lines with a new line plus the word `superseded` on the old line; do not erase.\n\nLine template:\n\n`[YYYY-MM-DD] <topic> | P=<0-2> | <observation> | <action>`\n\n`<topic>` is one lowercase tag you choose (`workflow`, `urgency`, `comms`, `safety`, ...). P is your urgency: 0 info, 1 affects deliverable, 2 time-critical.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Surface and files\n\nDiscover HTTP and paths only from the **brief** (plus what it points to for health checks). The brief also defines the workspace layout; **SKILL** stays read-only.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Writing discipline\n\nIdentifiers and quoted strings appear exactly as in Obs when they must be reproduced. Detail density drops as audience widens: peers get mechanics; leaders get risk and checkpoints; customers get impact and next touch without internals. Commitments need a clock time, bounded window, or owned checkpoint-never \"soon\" alone. One deliverable, one durable owner line, one next action; do not bundle unrelated threads.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Narration\n\nStay quiet for routine reads. Speak briefly only when the chain is long, the action irreversible, or the audience sensitive.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Wall clock\n\nRuns stop at a hard outer wall. Prioritize mandatory deliverables, batch reads, defer cosmetic exploration until the list is empty. If time is almost gone, emit a terse status: satisfied items, blocked items, blockers.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Final guard\n\nRe-read the **task prompt** before exit. The common failure mode is exceeding the ask. Stop when the list is satisfied or a stated blocker cannot be cleared with available access.\n\nUnless safety says otherwise, this sheet wins over defaults.\n"
